{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"Implement `TopModule` as a four-state Moore finite-state machine using registers for the current and next state, with `localparam` constants encoding states A, B, C, and D.\n\nUse a positive-edge-triggered state register with an active-high asynchronous reset. Its sensitivity list should respond to both the rising edge of `clk` and the rising edge of `areset`. When `areset` is asserted, immediately set the current state to A, independent of the clock. Otherwise, on each rising clock edge, load the computed next state.\n\nImplement combinational next-state logic with a `case` statement on the current state:\n\n- In A, remain in A when `in` is 0; transition to B when `in` is 1.\n- In B, transition to C when `in` is 0; remain in B when `in` is 1.\n- In C, transition to A when `in` is 0; transition to D when `in` is 1.\n- In D, transition to C when `in` is 0; transition to B when `in` is 1.\n\nAssign a safe default next state, preferably A, to handle an invalid or unknown state encoding and to avoid unintended combinational storage.\n\nBecause this is a Moore machine, derive `out` solely from the current registered state. Assert `out` only while the current state is D; drive it low in A, B, C, and invalid states. Resetting to A therefore makes the output 0 immediately after the asynchronous reset updates the state.\n\nInput sampling and transitions occur on rising clock edges. After an edge, the newly registered state determines the visible output for that cycle. Thus, an input bit that causes a transition into D makes `out` become 1 after that same rising edge. An input bit that causes a transition away from D makes `out` return to 0 after that edge. The transition rules naturally preserve overlapping input-pattern recognition, as demonstrated by the supplied traces."}}
